WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.708
Half of all sports searches happen while the game is live.

2
00:00:04.058 --> 00:00:10.257
And in 2025, 52% of fans used generative AI, up from 31%.

3
00:00:16.367 --> 00:00:19.865
Copilot already had the scores, the teams and the match state.

4
00:00:20.215 --> 00:00:22.164
But when fans asked about a game,

5
00:00:22.164 --> 00:00:24.172
the answer was still mostly text.

6
00:00:25.867 --> 00:00:26.946
The data was there.

7
00:00:27.296 --> 00:00:28.446
The interface wasn't.

8
00:00:30.833 --> 00:00:35.539
So I turned the structure already in the sports data into a visual hierarchy.

9
00:00:35.889 --> 00:00:38.354
The card answers the question at a glance,

10
00:00:38.354 --> 00:00:41.230
and deeper content waits for fans who want more.

11
00:00:43.333 --> 00:00:45.794
Cricket has overs, tennis has sets,

12
00:00:45.794 --> 00:00:48.325
F1 has laps, and the NFL has downs.

13
00:00:48.675 --> 00:00:51.822
One shared layer controls how every card reads,

14
00:00:51.822 --> 00:00:54.299
a sport layer controls what matters,

15
00:00:54.299 --> 00:00:58.048
and adding a sport became data mapping, not a redesign.

16
00:01:00.233 --> 00:01:03.860
Then I designed every state, not just the final score.

17
00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:06.298
Pre-game, live and post-game,

18
00:01:06.298 --> 00:01:09.177
plus the edge cases that break layouts,

19
00:01:09.177 --> 00:01:12.416
like rain delays, red flags and super overs.

20
00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:17.046
The system shipped across four sports,

21
00:01:17.046 --> 00:01:21.006
and others built Copilot's broader sports experience on top of it.

22
00:01:21.356 --> 00:01:24.869
When Copilot moved to a new design language in 2026,

23
00:01:24.869 --> 00:01:28.381
the same cards carried straight into the new shell.

24
00:01:31.067 --> 00:01:34.209
Consistency across surfaces is a system property,

25
00:01:34.209 --> 00:01:35.813
not a per-card decision.
